{"archetypes":[{"name":"bash-temp-file","focus_area":"code-robustness","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff replaces process-substitution find with mktemp+redirect in cleanup.sh — this pattern has temp-file leak risk on early exit and platform-specific mktemp behavior worth scrutinizing.","prompt_body":"Audit the mktemp + file-redirect pattern introduced in `skills/cleanup/scripts/cleanup.sh` for both the CACHE_DIR and TMP_ROOT passes. Check whether `rm -f \"$_cache_list\"` is guaranteed to run even when the script is killed (SIGINT/SIGTERM) or when `set -euo pipefail` causes an early exit inside the outer `if _cache_list=$(mktemp ...)` block — note the absence of an EXIT trap. Verify that `while IFS= read -r -d $'\\0' entry; done < \"$_cache_list\" || true` is semantically equivalent to the old `done < <(find ...) || true` process-substitution form, including NUL-terminator handling and the effect of `|| true` on loop-internal non-zero exits. Check whether `\"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/larch-cleanup-*.XXXXXX\"` as a mktemp template is Bash 3.2-compatible and whether the non-writable TMPDIR approach in the `mktemp-allocation-failure-warns` test case is portable across macOS and Linux (some platforms may use `/var/folders` or ignore TMPDIR). Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"flag-removal-completeness","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"--convergence-threshold is removed from run-step3-review.sh callers, but plan-review-loop.sh itself (not in the diff) may still parse or require the flag — a silent mismatch would cause argv-validation failures at runtime.","prompt_body":"Verify that the removal of `--convergence-threshold` from `run-step3-review.sh` and all call sites in the diff is complete and consistent with `plan-review-loop.sh`, which is NOT changed in the diff. Specifically: does `plan-review-loop.sh` still have a `--convergence-threshold` case in its argv parser? If yes, passing the flag would cause an unknown-option error; if no, the removal is clean. Also check `docs/configuration-and-permissions.md` (the `LARCH_DESIGN_CONVERGENCE_THRESHOLD` section is removed in the diff — confirm the contrast note at the end of `LARCH_DESIGN_ROUND_CAP` no longer references the removed var), `skills/design/references/plan-review.md`, and any other harness or doc file that the diff does not touch but that might still reference `LARCH_DESIGN_CONVERGENCE_THRESHOLD` or `--convergence-threshold`. Confirm the new `test-design-structure.sh` `absent` assertions (lines 241-242 in the diff) would catch any surviving references in SKILL.md and run-step3-review.sh. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"test-stub-precision","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The new write_stub_enum_failure checks for -mindepth in argv — if should_remove_by_age also uses -mindepth, the stub would accidentally fail the nested-scan path and produce misleading test results.","prompt_body":"Examine `write_stub_enum_failure` in `skills/cleanup/scripts/test-cleanup.sh` (added in the diff): it exits 2 when any argument equals `-mindepth`. Determine whether `should_remove_by_age` in `cleanup.sh` (the existing nested-activity scan that uses `-maxdepth 5`) also passes a `-mindepth` argument to `find`; if it does, the enum-failure stub would inadvertently fail the nested scan during the `enumeration-failure-warns` and `enumeration-failure-warns-tmp` test cases, which would cause those cases to both warn AND skip the nested-scan protection check — masking real behavior differences between enum-failure and scan-failure. Also verify that the `mktemp-allocation-failure-warns` test case (which uses `chmod 000` on TMPDIR) correctly reverts permissions with `chmod 755` before `unset TMPDIR` regardless of script exit, and that `CACHE_REMOVED` and `TMP_REMOVED` KVs are actually present in output when mktemp fails (the spec says cleanup still emits removal-count KVs). Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
